+# Render-mcp Restart Evidence Receipt — WO #2211 corroboration (deploy-plane timeline datum)
+
+Task ID: bridge-20261028-render2211-restart-evidence
+Refs: #2211 (P1-major, 3/5 Render services deployStatus update_failed), #2205 (frozen /health snapshot), #2201/#2179/#2146 (provider sentry series), #2195 (bridge_submit_patch outage)
+Lane: **report-only corroboration receipt.** Recovery actions named in #2211 (Render dashboard build-log inspection, fix-forward or retry deploy) are admin-lane; this receipt adds the one datum no other observer could hold: a first-hand timestamp for a render-mcp container restart inside the failure window. Zero mutation was performed on any tower/Render surface.
+
+## Why this receipt exists
+
+#2211's evidence (auditor cycle-20, 17:10-17:14Z) establishes the deploy-plane failure and rules out build-minute exhaustion, but the timeline lacks one fact: whether the failing services have been quietly serving stale builds, or whether containers have also been bouncing (reschedules/restart attempts). A container restart leaves a signature any long-lived tower CLIENT can see even without dashboard access: the tower's in-memory server state dies with the process. This agent holds exactly that vantage point — a tower session established in the early hours and used continuously across 10 hourly work cycles.
+
+## The datum: render-mcp restarted between 17:10:34Z and 17:16:50Z
+
+Two independent in-memory stores were lost in the same narrow window:
+
+| Signal | Last confirmed alive | First confirmed gone | Evidence |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| MCP session store | ~16:17Z (cycle-18 tower calls all green on session `384b17f1`, a >12h-old session) | 17:16:50Z (empty bodies on every tool) | Raw HTTP diagnostic at 17:17:33Z: HTTP 404, JSON body `session_not_found`, `recoverable: true, action: send-initialize` |
+| Health cache | 17:10:34Z (auditor's `system_health` read a populated cache: 18 healthy / 3 degraded / 0 unreachable) | 17:18:39Z (status tool: literal string "Health cache empty. Use scope 'health_live' for fresh sweep.") | Same in-memory loss class as the session store |
+
+Session TTL is ruled out as the cause: the expired session had lived >12 hours across 10+ hourly cycles of continuous use, and it died in the SAME window as the health cache — two co-located in-memory stores do not expire simultaneously by TTL coincidence. Process replacement explains both cleanly.
+
+Correlation with #2211's finding: render-mcp is one of the three services stuck on `update_failed`. A reschedule/restart in this window would bring the container back on the **last successful build** — exactly the stale-serving state #2211 describes, and exactly the mechanism #2211 predicts ("If a container is rescheduled, Render restarts the last successful deploy"). This receipt does not prove WHICH trigger fired (failed-deploy bounce vs platform reschedule vs manual restart); it proves a restart HAPPENED and brackets it to a 6-minute window the admin can match against Render's deploy event log.
+
+## Failure-mode documentation (fleet-facing): empty-body + error=False masks session death
+
+On session loss, every tower tool observed returned the SSE envelope with an empty payload while the client parse layer rendered it as **success with no content** — the same misleading signature class as the WAF silent-drop documented 2026-10-28 (cycle 13). A client that trusts `error=False` would conclude "queue is empty / feedback missing / health flat" instead of "transport dead". Recipe, now twice-proven:
+
+1. Any empty body on a previously-working tool = suspect transport, not data.
+2. One raw HTTP diagnostic against the endpoint exposes the truth (the 404 `session_not_found` JSON above).
+3. Recovery: single fresh initialize attempt restored a fully working session (new session `a9b17df1`, first attempt, no backoff needed) — the tower binary itself was healthy seconds after the restart, matching #2211's "runtime healthy, deploy plane broken" split.
+
+## Post-restart live sweep (17:20:52Z, scope health_live)
+
+- Provider-level: render, redis, github, supabase, cloudflare, infisical, vercel, kaggle, qdrant, stripe, firecrawl all healthy on fresh probes (consecutiveFailures=0). overallStatus: **down** driven by a single NEW incident.
+- NEW incident: **telegram transitioned to down at 17:21:03.648Z, "fetch failed"** — first provider DOWN in the sentry series' observation history (series baseline through gen-8 #2201: 7/8 alive, cascade all-green). Whether this is a transient sweep-plane fetch failure or a real provider event is for the next sentry pass to resolve (gen-9 due ~18:49Z by the sibling cadence).
+- Per-node detail (render-primary/worker/mcp trio): cache cold post-restart; the trio's degraded-with-fails=0 state will repopulate from fresh sweeps. The #1874-family interpretation rule (stable-degraded + fails=0 + edge-green = polarity; fails>0 or edge-red = REAL) applies unchanged to the repopulated readings.
+
+## Timeline synthesis for the admin
+
+- ~2026-10-24T08:32Z: last successful deploy generation on primary (per #2205's config-updatedAt datum; frozen /health corroborates).
+- 08:06Z → 17:10Z (this day): trio stably degraded with `update_failed`, fails=0 across at least 5 independent agent readings (08:06, 09:10, 10:18, 12:20, 17:10Z) — the failure was persistent, not oscillating.
+- (17:10:34Z, 17:16:50Z]: render-mcp container restart (THIS receipt's datum; session store + health cache wiped).
+- 17:17:50Z: client re-handshake clean on first attempt — container serving (old build) and responsive.
+- 17:20:52Z: provider sweep healthy; telegram incident NEW; per-node cache repopulating.
+- Net for #2211's recovery path: the restart does NOT change the fix (build-log inspection + fix-forward or platform-side retry remain admin-lane), but it tells the admin the fleet already took one uncontrolled bounce, and every tower client's session state fleet-wide was reset in that window — worth matching against Render's event log before choosing fix-forward vs retry.
+
